s the capital and largest city of Cape Verde, on the south coast of Santiago island.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/praja-mandal"><span class="enc-name">Praja Mandal</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">The Praja Mandals were popular movements in the princely states of British India from the 1920s to the 1940s, linked to the Indian National Congress through the All…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/prajadhipok"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/prajadhipok.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Prajadhipok</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Prajadhipok, King Rama VII of Siam, reigned from 1925 to 1935.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/prajnaparamita"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/prajnaparamita.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Prajnaparamita</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Prajnaparamita, or Perfection of Wisdom, sutras are a group of Mahayana Buddhist scriptures on the emptiness of all phenomena and the wisdom of the bodhisattva, the…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/prakrit"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/prakrit.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Prakrit</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Prakrit is the name for the vernacular Middle Indo-Aryan languages of ancient India, related to Sanskrit but simpler in sound and grammar.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/prasat-thong"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/prasat-thong.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Prasat Thong</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Prasat Thong was king of Ayutthaya from 1629 to 1656 and founder of the Prasat Thong dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/prasenajit"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/prasenajit.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Prasenajit</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Prasenajit, known in Pali as Pasenadi, was king of Kosala in northern India in the time of the Buddha, ruling from Shravasti.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/prasutagus"><span class="enc-name">Prasutagus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Prasutagus was king of the Iceni of East Anglia, who ruled as a client of Rome after the conquest of 43.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/pratap-chandra-bhanj-deo"><span class="enc-name">Pratap Chandra Bhanj Deo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pratap Chandra Bhanj Deo was the last ruling Maharaja of Mayurbhanj, a princely state in Odisha, from 1928 to 1949.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pratap-malla"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pratap-malla.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pratap Malla</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pratap Malla was king of Kantipur (Kathmandu) from 1641 to 1674, the most celebrated ruler of the Malla kingdoms of the Kathmandu valley.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pratap-singh-of-idar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pratap-singh-of-idar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pratap Singh of Idar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sir Pratap Singh was a Rathore prince of Jodhpur, long its chief minister and regent, who became Maharaja of Idar from 1902 to 1911.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pratap-singh-of-jammu-and-kashmir"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pratap-singh-of-jammu-and-kashmir.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pratap Singh of Jammu and Kashmir</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pratap Singh was the Dogra Maharaja of Jammu and Kashmir from 1885 to 1925.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pratap-singh-of-satara"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pratap-singh-of-satara.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pratap Singh of Satara</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pratap Singh was a descendant of Shivaji and nominal Chhatrapati of the Marathas under the Peshwas.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pratap-singh-prabhakar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pratap-singh-prabhakar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pratap Singh Prabhakar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pratap Singh Prabhakar was a Naruka Kachhwaha chief who broke away from Jaipur in the 1770s and founded the state of Alwar in north-eastern Rajasthan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/pratap-singha"><span class="enc-name">Pratap Singha</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pratap Singha, or Susenghphaa, was king of the Ahom kingdom of Assam from 1603 to 1641.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/prataparudra-kakatiya"><span class="enc-name">Prataparudra (Kakatiya dynasty)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Prataparudra was the last ruler of the Kakatiya dynasty, reigning from Warangal from 1289 to 1323 as the grandson and successor of Rudramadevi.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/prataparudra-deva"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/prataparudra-deva.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Prataparudra Deva</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Prataparudra Deva was the last great ruler of the Gajapati Kingdom of Odisha.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pratapgad"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pratapgad.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pratapgad</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Pratapgad is a hill fort in the Satara district of Maharashtra, western India, built in 1656 on the orders of Shivaji to guard the passes of the Western Ghats.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/pratapsinh-gaekwad"><span class="enc-name">Pratapsinh Gaekwad</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pratapsinh Gaekwad was the last ruling Maharaja of Baroda, succeeding his grandfather Sayajirao Gaekwad III in 1939.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/pratinas"><span class="enc-name">Pratinas</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pratinas of Phlius was a Greek dramatist and lyric poet active in Athens around 500 BCE, when he competed against Aeschylus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/prato-cathedral"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/prato-cathedral.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Prato Cathedral</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Prato Cathedral, dedicated to Santo Stefano, grew from the parish church around which the town developed and was enlarged from the 12th to the 15th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/prattigau"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/prattigau.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Prättigau</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Prättigau is an Alpine valley of the Landquart river in the canton of Graubünden, eastern Switzerland.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/pravahana-jaivali"><span class="enc-name">Pravahana Jaivali</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pravahana Jaivali was a king of Panchala who appears in the Chandogya and Brihadaranyaka Upanishads.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pravarapura"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pravarapura.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pravarapura</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Pravarapura was a capital of the main branch of the Vakataka dynasty in central India, founded by King Pravarasena II in the 5th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/pravarasena-i"><span class="enc-name">Pravarasena I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pravarasena I was the son of Vindhyashakti and the second ruler of the Vakataka dynasty of the Deccan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pravarasena-ii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pravarasena-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pravarasena II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pravarasena II was a 5th-century king of the main branch of the Vakataka dynasty in the Deccan, son of Rudrasena II and the Gupta princess Prabhavatigupta.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pravia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pravia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pravia</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Pravia is a town in western Asturias, near the mouth of the Nalón.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/pravir-chandra-bhanj-deo"><span class="enc-name">Pravir Chandra Bhanj Deo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pravir Chandra Bhanj Deo was the last ruling Maharaja of Bastar, installed as a child in 1936.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/praxedes-mateo-sagasta"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/praxedes-mateo-sagasta.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Práxedes Mateo Sagasta</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Práxedes Mateo Sagasta was a Spanish politician and leader of the Liberal Party during the Bourbon Restoration.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/praxiteles"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/praxiteles.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Praxiteles</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Praxiteles was an Athenian sculptor of the 4th century BCE, one of the most admired artists of ancient Greece.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/prayagraj.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Prayagraj</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Prayagraj, called Allahabad from the time of Akbar until 2018, is a city in Uttar Pradesh, India, at the confluence of the Ganges and Yamuna, a sacred bathing place for…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/prazero"><span class="enc-name">Prazero</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">A prazero was the holder of a prazo, a Portuguese crown land grant in the Zambezi valley, often a powerful estate lord with private armed forces.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/prazo"><span class="enc-name">Prazo</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">A prazo was a large estate in the Zambezi valley granted by the Portuguese crown, typically on lease for several generations.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/pre-columbian-era"><span class="enc-name">Pre-Columbian era</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">The pre-Columbian era refers to the history of the Americas before the arrival of Christopher Columbus in 1492 and subsequent European colonisation.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/prefect"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/prefect.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Prefect</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">A prefect is an official placed in charge of an administrative unit or service.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/prefect-of-egypt"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/prefect-of-egypt.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Prefect of Egypt</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The prefect of Egypt (praefectus Aegypti) was the governor of the Roman province of Egypt from its annexation in 30 BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pregolya"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/pregolya.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pregolya</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Pregolya is a river in Russia's Kaliningrad Oblast, formed by the junction of the Instruch and the Angrapa and flowing about 123 km west to the Vistula Lagoon.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/prekmurje"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/prekmurje.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Prekmurje</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><